{
  "raw": {
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Main-Campus/Adjunct-Pool--Biology_JR100030": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty Pool - Biology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-27\nPart time\n\nJob Description: About the Department of Biology: The Biology Department at Seattle Pacific University is a place where students and faculty engage collaboratively in constructing and refining their understanding of the biological world. All biology courses emphasize the active participation of students in the learning process. We prioritize a highly interactive community of learning both inside and outside the classroom. Job Description: We typically have openings for adjunct biology faculty in multiple sections of non-majors biology, introductory biology, and anatomy & physiology. Teaching in these courses would provide an opportunity to work closely with other SPU faculty, with the intention of integrating authentic research experiences for students into the general biology lab curriculum. Requirements: Master's degree in biology or relevant field Bachelor's degree applicants will be considered Commitment to support active, participatory learning experiences Personal commitment to Christian faith, enthusiasm about supporting   SPU's mission , alignment with the  University's Statement of Faith , and willingness to accept the lifestyle standards of the community. Additional Information: Compensation: Starts at $900 per credit The pool of applications at this site will be accessed periodically as specific teaching needs arise. Please complete the online application in Workday. Be prepared to upload a cover letter and CV/resume. Please direct inquiries to: Dr. Cara Wall-Scheffler, Ph.D. Professor and Co-Chair of Biology 206-281-2201 cwallsch@spu.edu Dr. Jenny Tenlen, Ph.D. Associate Professor and Co-Chair of Biology (206) 281-2007 tenlenj@spu.edu"
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Main-Campus/Assistant-Rowing--Coach-II--Part-time--10-12-hours-per-week--_JR100347": {
      "title": "Assistant Rowing  Coach II (Part-time, 10-12 hours per week))",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-23\nPart time\n\nJob Description: Who We Are Seattle Pacific University is a Christian university fully committed to engaging the culture and changing the world by graduating people of competence and character, becoming people of wisdom, and modeling grace-filled community. Seattle Pacific Athletics is committed to developing champions in competition, in the classroom, and in life. As a member of NCAA Division II and the Great Northwest Athletic Conference (GNAC), SPU Athletics emphasizes excellence, integrity, service, and Christian character. The Women's Rowing program provides student-athletes with a highly competitive and supportive environment where they can grow as athletes, students, leaders, and followers of Christ. What We Need: Seattle Pacific University is seeking an enthusiastic and driven individual to join our Women’s Rowing team as a part-time Assistant Coach. This role is ideal for someone eager to grow within NCAA rowing while playing a pivotal role in recruiting, mentoring, and leading student-athletes toward competitive success. The ideal candidate will be a strong communicator, team player, and positive role model who thrives in a faith-centered educational environment. The Assistant Rowing Coach position supports Seattle Pacific University’s mission by contributing to a Christ‑centered learning and working community committed to academic excellence, service, and engagement with the world. In this role, the employee brings their skills, gifts, and professional expertise to work that matters—serving students, colleagues, and the broader University community in ways that reflect SPU’s values and purpose. To that end, all University employees are required to be aligned with and supportive of SPU's Statement of Faith and its derivative policies and lifestyle expectations. What You’ll Do: Lead, mentor, and support student-athletes with integrity and enthusiasm Recruit prospective student-athletes both from high schools and from the on-campus community Instruct rowing fundamentals and foster development of novice rowers into varsity contributors Collaborate with the Head Coach and other staff to deliver safe, successful team experiences Assist with administrative tasks and logistical operations Participate in spring travel and weekend events as required Help with rowing equipment maintenance and boathouse upkeep Represent SPU Athletics with professionalism and positivity   What You Bring: Education:  Bachelor’s degree required Experience:  Experience with a competitive collegiate rowing program (as athlete or coach) Skills & Characteristics: Strong interpersonal and leadership abilities Initiative and commitment to athlete development and recruitment Enthusiasm for collaborative team culture and student mentorship Interest in learning and growing as a collegiate coach Certifications (or willingness to obtain post-hire with SPU support): CPR Certification Washington State Boater’s License Washington State Driver’s License USRowing Level 2 Certification   Mission Alignment:  You have a personal commitment to Christian faith, enthusiasm about supporting SPU's mission , alignment with the University's Statement of Faith , and willingness to accept the lifestyle standards of the Christian community. Salary Range: $8,000 - $12,000 annually, DOE, 10 month appt Position Type: Part-Time, Exempt Hours: Approx. 10-12 hours per week; mornings preferred Start Date: Mid September End Date: Mid June Why SPU? Seattle Pacific University’s beautiful campus is nestled in North Queen Anne, just minutes from downtown Seattle. The Women’s Rowing program enjoys direct access to our on-campus boathouse and daily practices on the protected waters of the Seattle Ship Canal and Lake Union. SPU’s rowing team has experienced significant growth and success and is poised for continued excellence with institutional support and a strong vision for the future. Additional Information: Ready to Apply? Submit your application online through Workday, including a cover letter and resume. We look forward to hearing how you can contribute to the success and spirit of SPU Rowing."
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Main-Campus/Assistant-Rowing-Coach-I--Part-time--19-hours-per-week-_JR100344": {
      "title": "Assistant Rowing Coach I (Part-time, 19 hours per week)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-23\nPart time\n\nJob Description: Who We Are Seattle Pacific University is a Christian university fully committed to engaging the culture and changing the world by graduating people of competence and character, becoming people of wisdom, and modeling grace-filled community. Seattle Pacific Athletics is committed to developing champions in competition, in the classroom, and in life. As a member of NCAA Division II and the Great Northwest Athletic Conference (GNAC), SPU Athletics emphasizes excellence, integrity, service, and Christian character. The Women's Rowing program provides student-athletes with a highly competitive and supportive environment where they can grow as athletes, students, and leaders. What We Need: Seattle Pacific University is seeking an enthusiastic and driven individual to join our Women’s Rowing team as a part-time Assistant Coach. This role is ideal for someone eager to grow within NCAA rowing while playing a pivotal role in recruiting, mentoring, and leading student-athletes toward competitive success. The ideal candidate will be a strong communicator, team player, and positive role model who thrives in a faith-centered educational environment. The Assistant Rowing Coach position supports Seattle Pacific University’s mission by contributing to a Christ‑centered learning and working community committed to academic excellence, service, and engagement with the world. In this role, the employee brings their skills, gifts, and professional expertise to work that matters—serving students, colleagues, and the broader University community in ways that reflect SPU’s values and purpose. To that end, all University employees are required to be aligned with and supportive of SPU's Statement of Faith and its derivative policies and lifestyle expectations. What You’ll Do: Lead, mentor, and support student-athletes with integrity and enthusiasm Recruit prospective student-athletes both from high schools and from the on-campus community Instruct rowing fundamentals and foster development of novice rowers into varsity contributors Collaborate with the Head Coach and other staff to deliver safe, successful team experiences Assist with administrative tasks and logistical operations Participate in spring travel and weekend events as required Help with rowing equipment maintenance and boathouse upkeep Represent SPU Athletics with professionalism and positivity   What You Bring: Education:  Bachelor’s degree required Experience:  Experience with a competitive collegiate rowing program (as athlete or coach) Skills & Characteristics: Strong interpersonal and leadership abilities Initiative and commitment to athlete development and recruitment Enthusiasm for collaborative team culture and student mentorship Interest in learning and growing as a collegiate coach Certifications (or willingness to obtain post-hire with SPU support): CPR Certification Washington State Boater’s License Washington State Driver’s License USRowing Level 2 Certification   Mission Alignment:  You have a personal commitment to Christian faith, enthusiasm about supporting SPU's mission , alignment with the University's Statement of Faith , and willingness to accept the lifestyle standards of the Christian community. ​ Appointment Details: Salary Range: $25,000 - $31,000 annually for 10 month appt, DOE Position Type: Part-Time, Exempt Hours: Approx. 19 hours per week; mornings preferred Start Date: Mid September End Date: Mid June Why SPU? Seattle Pacific University’s beautiful campus is nestled in North Queen Anne, just minutes from downtown Seattle. The Women’s Rowing program enjoys direct access to our on-campus boathouse and daily practices on the protected waters of the Seattle Ship Canal and Lake Union. SPU’s rowing team has experienced significant growth and success and is poised for continued excellence with institutional support and a strong vision for the future. Additional Information: Ready to Apply? Submit your application online through Workday, including a cover letter and resume. We look forward to hearing how you can contribute to the success and spirit of SPU Rowing."
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Main-Campus/Adjunct-Faculty-Pool---Chemistry-Biochemistry_JR100337": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty Pool - Chemistry/Biochemistry",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-23\nPart time\n\nJob Description: Who We Are Seattle Pacific University is a Christian university fully committed to engaging the culture and changing the world by graduating people of competence and character, becoming people of wisdom, and modeling grace-filled community. About the Chemistry and Biochemistry Program (CBP): The CBP is a member of the Division of Natural Sciences of the College of Applied and Natural Sciences (CANS) at Seattle Pacific University (SPU) Division of Natural Sciences Mission Statement: Promoting scientific learning as a way of understanding Creation The CBP offers a Bachelor of Science degree in Biochemistry and a minor in Chemistry.  The CBP mission and values include developing students to be ethical practitioners of science who uphold deep integrity in academics, experimental design, and data interpretation. The program prepares students to better understand the world around them and find their place in upholding meaningful relationships between themselves, their work, and nature.   We typically have openings for adjunct chemistry faculty in laboratory sections of General Chemistry (CHM 1211L, CHM 1212L), Survey of General Chemistry (CHM 1310L), Survey of Organic Chemistry (CHM 1330L), and Survey of Biochemistry (CHM 1360L). Teaching in these courses would provide an opportunity to work closely with other SPU faculty, with the intention of promoting scientific integrity and excellent work ethic throughout the laboratory experiment experiences for students in the 1000-level chemistry lab curriculum.   Requirements: ·    PhD or Masters degree in a branch of chemistry (or related field).  ·    Bachelors degree applicants will be considered if they have teaching experience. ·    Commitment to support active, participatory learning experiences. ·     Personal commitment to Christian faith, enthusiasm about supporting   SPU's mission , alignment with the   University's Statement of Faith , and willingness to accept the lifestyle standards of the community.   Compensation:  $900   per credit     Please direct inquiries to: Karisa Pierce, Ph.D. Professor of Chemistry and Co-Chair of Chemistry and Biochemistry Program (206) 281-2102 piercek@spu.edu Cara Wall-Scheffler, Ph.D. Division Lead of the Natural Sciences 206-281-2201 cwallsch@spu.edu   Additional Information: The pool of applications at this site will be accessed periodically as specific teaching needs arise. Please complete the online application in Workday. Be prepared to upload a cover letter and CV/resume."
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Main-Campus/Adjunct-Faculty--Theology-and-Ministerial-Education_JR100180": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty Pool - Theology and Ministerial Education",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-23\nPart time\n\nJob Description: About Seattle Pacific Seminary Seattle Pacific Seminary is a workshop of the Holy Spirit that exists to pursue the knowledge of the Triune God for the deepening of the church’s worship and the strengthening of its love for the least of these. Rooted in the Wesleyan tradition, the Seminary seeks to educate, equip, and empower students to participate in the reconciling work of Christ for the renewal of the church and the life of the world. Through rigorous theological study, spiritual formation, and practical engagement, the Seminary prepares students for faithful service in pastoral ministry, nonprofit leadership, chaplaincy, and other expressions of Christian vocation. Our community values intellectual inquiry, spiritual vitality, and a deep commitment to justice and reconciliation in the world. Job Description Seattle Pacific Seminary is accepting applications for adjunct faculty in theological and ministerial education. Teaching opportunities include courses in biblical studies, church history, theology, ethics, spiritual formation, preaching, and ministry leadership at both the graduate and undergraduate levels.  Teaching in these courses provides an opportunity to engage students intellectually and spiritually, fostering integration of academic study, vocational calling, and personal faith development within a dynamic learning community. Requirements Masters or Doctoral degree in theology, biblical studies, church history, practical theology, or a closely related field. Demonstrated excellence in teaching and mentoring students from diverse backgrounds and traditions. Commitment to integrative, interdisciplinary learning, uniting theological scholarship with the liberal arts and ethical engagement in the world. Experience with or openness to alternative learning systems, including hybrid and online teaching modalities. Personal commitment to Christian faith, enthusiasm about supporting SPU’s mission , alignment with the University’s Statement of Faith , and willingness to uphold the lifestyle standards of the SPU community. Desire to meet regularly with students for mentoring and vocational discernment. Additional Information: Compensation:  Commensurate with rank, qualifications, and experience. Adjunct compensation typically ranges from $850–$1,000 per credit hour. Application Instructions: To apply, complete the online application through SPU’s Workday system. Prepare to upload a cover letter and CV/Resume . The applicant pool at this site will be reviewed periodically as instructional needs arise across seminary disciplines. For further inquiries, please contact: Seattle Pacific Seminary Office: Seminary@spu.edu Additional Information: We welcome your application and the opportunity to hear how your talents, perspective, and passions can contribute to SPU’s mission: to engage the culture and change the world through the gospel of Jesus Christ , preparing students for lives of service, leadership, and meaning."
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Marriage-and-Family-Therapy-Adjunct-Faculty-Pool_JR100185": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty Pool - Marriage and Family Therapy",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-23\nPart time\n\nJob Description: The College of Applied and Natural Science: Division of Health and Behavioral Sciences at Seattle Pacific University offers faith integrated academic programs in  Clinical Psychology (PhD) , Industrial and Organizational Psychology ( MA  and  PhD ),  Marriage and Family Therapy (MS) , Psychology ( BA / BS / MS ), and  Medical Family Therapy (Professional/post-graduate) . All programs emphasize the integration of practice, theory, research, and professional preparation for psychology-relevant careers in clinical and organizational contexts. The masters program in Marriage and Family Therapy as well as Medical Family Therapy offers opportunities for adjunct instructors. Courses vary by topic and academic term, and application review occurs continuously as needs arise. If you are interested in specific areas of teaching and/or courses, please include this information in your cover letter. Requirements: Master's or doctoral degree in area of discipline. A personal commitment to Christian faith, enthusiasm about supporting  SPU's mission , alignment with the  University's Statement of Faith , and willingness to accept the lifestyle standards of the Christian community. Additional Information: Applications will be reviewed on a regular basis, as needs arise. Compensation is $1000 per credit for masters level courses. Application Instructions: Please complete the online application. Please be prepared to upload a resume/CV and cover letter. Also completed as part of the SPU application for adjuncts will be a one page statement responding to the Statement of Faith and teaching philosophy. For questions and inquiries, please contact: Peter Rivera, Associate Professor of Psychology Program Lead: Marriage and Family Therapy 206-281-2632, riverap@spu.edu Additional Information: We would love to hear how your passions and talents can contribute to our mission to engage the culture and help to bring about positive change in the world!"
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Seattle-WA/Apparel-Design-and-Merchandising-Adjunct-Faculty-Pool_JR100012": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty Pool - Apparel Design and Merchandising",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-23\nPart time\n\nJob Description: SPU's Apparel Design and Merchandising Program is an area in the College of Business and Technology that offers undergraduate majors in Apparel Design, Apparel Merchandising, and a minor in Apparel Design and Merchandising, provides students with excellent preparation for professional careers in the industry. Our program is committed to building an excellent and diverse faculty who desire to be part of a supportive Christian community in which students are prepared to become responsible and effective servant-leaders. What we need: Adjunct faculty position in apparel design and merchandising. Courses: Apparel Design, Basic Sewing, Flat Pattern, Draping, Fashion Illustration, Fashion Show Production, Visual Merchandising, Apparel Production, Retailing, Retail Mathematics, Global Sourcing, CAD Design, Portfolio, History of Fashion, Appearance and Culture, Textiles. Responsibilities: Teach a course including grading and holding office hours.  Requirements: Preferred master's degree or PhD in a relevant field. Bachelor's degree applicants will be considered if accompanied with significant industry (+ 5 years) and/or teaching experience. A personal commitment to Christian faith, enthusiasm about supporting  SPU's mission , alignment with the  University's Statement of Faith , and willingness to accept the lifestyle standards of the Christian community. Compensation: $800 - $950 per credit, DOE Additional Information: To Apply: Complete the online application through the Workday Careers site. Please attach a resume/CV and cover letter in the online application process. Inquiries relation to apparel merchandising and design courses should be addressed to: Gwia Kim, Assistant Professor of Apparel Design and Merchandising Program College of Business and Technoloty 206-281-2465, gkim@spu.edu"
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Main-Campus/Adjunct-Faculty--Division-of-Technology--Engineering-Comp-Science-Physics-Math-_JR100139": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty Pool - Engineering/Computer Science/Data Science/Math/Physics",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-23\nPart time\n\nJob Description: About the Division of Technology within the College of Business and Technology The College of Business and Technology (CBT) consists of the Division of Business and Government (DBG) and the Division of Technology (DT). The faculty and staff at DT serve undergraduate majors in Electrical Engineering, Mechanical Engineering, General Engineering, Biomedical Engineering, Data Science, and Computer Science; furthermore, this division houses the Physics and Mathematics faculty. The division is committed to providing a high-quality education through the lens of Christian faith and values and is distinguished by its emphasis on applied learning. All engineering programs are ABET-accredited (with the exception of Biomedical Engineering, which the CBT has started to offer in 2025). What we need: Thank you for your inquiry about an adjunct teaching opportunity at the Division of Technology of the College of Business and Technology at SPU. Adjunct educators are hired on a contractual, part-time basis, teaching courses for specified quarters throughout an academic year. Duties include developing syllabi and assignments, teaching classes, holding office hours, and assessing student performance. The pool of applications at this site will be accessed periodically as specific needs arise in all areas of Engineering, Data Science, Computer Science, Mathematics, Statistics, and Physics. We appreciate candidates qualified to teach in advanced areas of our disciplines who bring industry experience to the classroom. The salary range for Adjunct Professors in CBT is $950 - 1,250 per credit hour, depending on education and experience. What you bring: Master’s degree or Ph.D. in a relevant field. Bachelor’s degree applicants will be considered if accompanied by significant industry and/or teaching experience. A personal commitment to Christian faith, enthusiasm about supporting SPU’s mission, alignment with the University’s Statement of Faith , and a willingness to accept the lifestyle expectations of the Christian community. All applicants are expected to provide a statement of faith in their application. Direct inquiries to: The Division of Technology, dtleads@spu.edu Please  do not  submit application materials to this email address.  Only applications submitted through our online application process will be considered. Additional Information: Application Instructions To apply, submit the online application in Workday. Be prepared to upload a cover letter, CV/Resume, faith statement, and teaching philosophy."
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Seattle-WA/Writing-Instructor-Adjunct-Pool_JR100013": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty Pool - Writing Instructors",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-23\nPart time\n\nJob Description: About the Writing Program:  The Writing Program at Seattle Pacific University supports writing instruction from a student's first year until graduation. In addition to coordinating the two-course sequence all incoming first-year students take at SPU (the first housed in the English department and the second taught by faculty across campus), the Writing Program offers a seminar on the teaching of writing, hosts writing/pedagogy workshops, facilitates faculty writing groups, and oversees the year-end Rauer and Hedges Writing Awards and their companion \"How I Learned to Write\" lecture series. More information can be found at  spu.edu/writing . We often have staffing needs in WRI 1000 and WRI 1100, our two-course required first-year writing sequence.  Teaching in these courses would provide an opportunity to work closely with other SPU WRI faculty in our continued efforts to engage students in a transformative first-year experience.  Requirements: Doctoral degree in composition and rhetoric, or in literature, or Terminal degree in related field, or Terminal degree in another field with heavy emphasis on writing A personal commitment to Christian faith, enthusiasm about supporting  SPU's mission , alignment with the  University's Statement of Faith , and willingness to accept the lifestyle standards of the Christian community. A c omfortability discussing the intersection of faith and writing with students Additional Information: To apply: Please submit the online application through the Workday Careers site and upload a resume/CV and cover letter. The salary for adjunct faculty is $782/credit in the College of Arts and Sciences. Inquireis may be directed to Dr. Traynor Hansen, Director of Campus Writing, thansen3@spu.edu ."
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Main-Campus/Undergraduate-Psycholgy-Adjunct-Instructor-Pool_JR100196": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty Pool - Undergraduate Psychology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-23\nPart time\n\nJob Description: About the Division of Applied and Behavioral Sciences: The Division of Applied and Behavioral Sciences at Seattle Pacific University offers faith integrated academic programs in  Clinical Psychology (PhD) , Industrial and Organizational Psychology ( MA  and  PhD ),  Marriage and Family Therapy (MS) , Psychology ( BA / BS / MS ), and  Medical Family Therapy (Professional/post-graduate) . All programs emphasize the integration of practice, theory, research, and professional preparation for psychology-relevant careers in clinical and organizational contexts.   The undergraduate Psychology program offers occasional opportunities for adjunct instructors. Courses vary by topic and academic term, and application review occurs continuously as needs arise. If you are interested in specific areas of teaching and/or courses, please include this information in your cover letter. Requirements: Master's or doctoral degree in area of discipline. A personal commitment to Christian faith, enthusiasm about supporting  SPU's mission , alignment with the  u niversity's Statement of Faith , and willingness to accept the lifestyle standards of the Christian community. Additional Information: Applications will be reviewed on a regular basis, as needs arise. Compensation: $1000 per credit for adjuncts at the masters level and $1200 per credit for individuals with a PhD.   Application Instructions: Please complete the online application, uploading a cover letter and a CV. As a part of the application you will also be prompted to write a one-page response to the university's Statement of Faith and a statement of your teaching philosophy.   For questions and inquiries, please contact: Phil Baker, PhD Program Lead, Undergraduate Psychology 206-281-2178, bakerp5@spu.edu Additional Information: We look forward to hearing about the unique contributions you can make with our students and in the SPU community!"
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Seattle-WA/Clinical-Psychology-Doctoral-Program--Adjunct-Pool_JR100015": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty Pool - Clinical Psychology Doctoral Program",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-23\nPart time\n\nJob Description: About the School of Psychology, Family, and Community:   The School of Psychology, Family, and Community at Seattle Pacific University offers faith integrated academic programs in   Clinical Psychology (PhD) , Industrial and Organizational Psychology ( MA   and   PhD ),   Marriage and Family Therapy (MS) , Psychology ( BA / BS / MS ), and   Medical Family Therapy (Professional/post-graduate) . All programs emphasize the integration of practice, theory, research, and professional preparation for psychology-relevant careers in clinical and organizational contexts. The doctoral program for Clinical Psychology offers opportunities for adjunct instructors. Courses vary by topic and academic term, and application review occurs continuously as needs arise. If you are interested in specific areas of teaching and/or courses, please include this information in your cover letter. Requirements: Master's or doctoral degree in area of discipline. A personal commitment to Christian faith, enthusiasm about supporting  SPU's mission , alignment with the  University's Statement of Faith , and willingness to accept the lifestyle standards of the Christian community. Additional Information: Compensation: $1000 - $1200 per credit Applications will be reviewed on a rolling basis, as needs arise For questions and inquiries, please contact: Jenny Lee Vaydich, Associate Professor of Psychology Chair of Research Psychology (MS) and Clinical Psychology (PhD) 206-281-2872, vaydichj@spu.edu Additional Information: Application Instructions: Please complete the online application, and be prepared to upload a CV and cover letter."
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Main-Campus/Adjunct-Faculty-Pool--Lydia-Green-Nursing-Program_JR100029": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty Pool - Lydia Green Nursing Program",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-23\nPart time\n\nJob Description: About the Lydia Green Nursing Program: The Lydia Green Nursing Program is a part of the College of Applied and Natural Sciences (CANS) and includes a vibrant undergraduate BSN program. Our nursing program is set apart by the integration of robust academics and relationship-centered learning that is grounded in Christian faith and values. The program and our graduates are highly regarded by the surrounding regional healthcare community. The nursing programs is approved by the Washington State Board of Nursing (WABON) and accredited by the Commission on Collegiate Nursing Education (CCNE). Job Description: We are accepting applications for adjunct faculty for clinical teaching in the following areas: Undergraduate, pre-licensure: acute care (adults), pediatrics, obstetrics, community health and psych-mental health. Teaching in these courses provides an opportunity to work closely with students in the clinical setting, with preceptors, and other SPU faculty to ensure transformative clinical learning experiences. Requirements: Master's degree in nursing or closely related field. Unencumbered license as registered nurse in the State of Washington. For undergraduate clinical teaching, BSN applicants with a minimum of 3 years of experience in the clinical subject area will be considered. Commitment to support active, participatory learning experiences. Personal commitment to Christian faith, enthusiasm about supporting   SPU's mission , alignment with the  University's Statement of Faith , and willingness to accept the lifestyle standards of the community. Additional Information: Compensation: BSN prepared = $1,875 per credit, Master's prepared = $2,100 per credit, Doctoral prepared $2,250 per credit Application Instructions: To apply, submit the on-line application in Workday. Be prepared to upload a cover letter and CV/Resume. The pool of applications at this site will be accessed periodically as specific needs arise in all areas of clinical nursing. For inquiries and additional information, contact: Dr. Antwinett O. Lee Interim Chief Nurse Administrator, Lydia Green Nursing Program Seattle Pacific University 3307 3rd Ave W, Ste 335 Seattle, WA 98119 Ph: (206) 795-8528 Email: leea30@spu.edu"
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Main-Campus/Adjunct-Faculty-Pool--Division-of-Business-and-Government_JR100088": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty Pool - Division of Business and Government",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-23\nPart time\n\nJob Description: About the Division of Business and Government within the College of Business and Technology:  The College of Business and Technology consists of the Division of Business and Government (DBG) and the Division of Technology. The faculty and staff at DBG serve undergraduate majors in Accounting, Apparel Design and Merchandising, Business Administration, Economics, Political Science, and Politics, Philosophy and Economics (an interdisciplinary program), as well as a master's degree in Data Analytics in Business (MS; STEM) and Graduate Certificates. The division is committed to providing a high-quality education through the lens of Christian faith and values and is distinguished by its emphasis on applied learning. It was the first within the Council for Christian Colleges and Universities membership to achieve secondary accreditation with the AACSB (in 2000) and successfully maintained accreditation in 2025. In addition, the College of Business and Technology was the first adopter within the Northwest Region of the UN's Principles for Responsible Management Education (PRME) and is a PRME Champion School. Beyond such quality indicators, the faculty and staff within the division are a supportive community marked by collaborative teaching, scholarship, and service.     Job Description: Thank you for your inquiry about an adjunct teaching opportunity at the College of Business and Technology at SPU. Adjunct educators are hired on a contractual, part-time basis, teaching introductory undergraduate or preparatory courses for specified quarters throughout an academic year. Duties include developing syllabi and assignments, teaching classes and assessing student performance. The pool of applications at this site will be accessed periodically as specific needs arise in all areas of Business, Ethics and Pre-Law, Economics, Accounting, Political Science, and Global Development Studies.  The salary range for Adjuct Professors in SBGE is $950 - 1,250 per credit hour, depending on education and experience. Requirements: Master's degree or Ph.D. in a relevant field. Bachelor's degree applicants will be considered if accompanied by significant industry and/or teaching experience. A personal commitment to Christian faith, enthusiasm about supporting SPU's mission , alignment with the University's Statement of Faith , and a willingness to accept the lifestyle standards of the Christian community. If you have any questions, please email SBGE@spu.edu.   Please  do not  submit application materials to this email address.  Only applications submitted through our online application process will be considered. Additional Information: Application Instructions: To apply, submit the on-line application in Workday. Be prepared to upload a cover letter and CV/Resume."
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Main-Campus/Industrial-Organizational-Psychology-Adjunct-Faculty-Pool_JR100284": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty Pool - Industrial-Organizational Psychology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-23\nPart time\n\nJob Description: Part of the College of Applied and Natural Sciences , The Division of Health and Behavioral Sciences offers faith integrated academic programs in Industrial and Organizational Psychology ( MA and PhD ), Clinical Psychology (PhD), Marriage and Family Therapy (MS), Psychology (BA/BS/MS), and Medical Family Therapy (Professional/post-graduate). All programs emphasize the integration of practice, theory, research, and professional preparation for psychology-relevant careers in clinical and organizational contexts.   The masters and doctoral program for Industrial-Organizational Psychology offers occasional opportunities for adjunct instructors. Courses vary by topic and academic term, and application review occurs continuously as needs arise. If you are interested in specific areas of teaching and/or courses, please include this information in your cover letter.   Requirements: Master's or doctoral degree in area of discipline. A personal commitment to Christian faith, enthusiasm about supporting SPU's mission , alignment with the University's Statement of Faith , and willingness to accept the lifestyle standards of the Christian community. Additional Information: Applications will be reviewed on a regular basis, as needs arise.   Compensation: $900-$1200 per credit   Additional Information: Application Instructions: Please be prepared to upload a cover letter and CV. As you complete the online application, you will also be instructed to submit one-page statement of faith and teaching philosophy. For questions and inquiries, please contact: Helen Chung, Associate Professor of Industrial-Organizational Psychology Chair of Industrial-Organizational Psychology helenchung@spu.edu"
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Main-Campus/Executive-Assistant--University-Advancement_JR100320": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant, University Advancement",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-17\nFull time\n\nJob Description: Who We Are Seattle Pacific University is a Christian university fully committed to engaging the culture and changing the world by graduating people of competence and character, becoming people of wisdom, and modeling grace-filled community. University Advancement supports the mission of SPU by fostering meaningful relationships with alumni, parents, donors, churches, foundations, and friends of the university. Through fundraising, donor stewardship, alumni engagement, and strategic partnerships, University Advancement helps secure the resources that support student success, academic excellence, and the long-term vitality of the university.   What We Need Seattle Pacific University is seeking an experienced and highly professional   Executive Assistant  to provide executive administrative and operational support to the Vice President for University Advancement (VPUA) and the Office of University Advancement. The Executive Assistant position supports Seattle Pacific   University’s mission  by contributing to a Christ‑centered learning and working community committed to academic excellence, service, and engagement with the world. In this role, the employee brings their skills, gifts, and professional expertise to work that matters—serving students, colleagues, and the broader University community in ways that reflect SPU’s values and purpose. To that end, all University employees are required to be aligned with and supportive of   SPU's Statement of Faith  and its derivative policies and lifestyle expectations. This role serves as a trusted partner to the Vice President and a key representative of the Advancement Office. The Executive Assistant supports executive-level operations, donor engagement activities, trustee committee coordination, event planning, budget administration, office management, and cross-campus collaboration. The ideal candidate is exceptionally organized, relationship-oriented, and able to manage competing priorities with discretion, professionalism, and attention to detail. This position regularly interacts with university leaders, trustees, donors, alumni, faculty, staff, students, and community partners, requiring excellent communication skills and the ability to handle confidential information with sound judgment and integrity. This role offers a unique opportunity to support donor engagement, philanthropic partnerships, and university advancement initiatives while working closely with senior university leadership. This is an   on-campus position based in Seattle, WA  and is not eligible for remote work. What You'll Do Executive & Administrative Support Provide high-level executive administrative and operational support to the Vice President for University Advancement Manage complex calendars, scheduling, logistics, travel arrangements, and follow-up activities Prepare briefing materials, reports, presentations, meeting agendas, and correspondence Coordinate communication and collaboration between Advancement leadership and campus partners Support Advancement leadership meetings, special projects, donor engagement efforts, and departmental initiatives Donor Portfolio & Relationship Management Support Assist the Vice President in managing donor engagement activities and relationship tracking Maintain accurate records of donor interactions, meetings, communications, and follow-up actions Coordinate donor engagement logistics and stewardship activities on behalf of the Vice President Monitor donor follow-up commitments and help ensure timely completion of action items Assist in maintaining donor records and supporting donor moves-management processes Help prepare donor briefing materials, visit summaries, engagement reports, and follow-up communications Collaborate with Development and Engagement teams to support donor relationship strategies and priorities Advancement Committee & Trustee Support Coordinate logistics, meeting materials, agendas, and communications for Advancement-related Board committees and trustee engagement activities Prepare reports, presentations, and supporting materials for Board and committee meetings Attend assigned committee meetings, record minutes, track action items, and coordinate follow-up activities Maintain committee records, trustee engagement information, and governance-related documentation Support communication and logistics for trustees participating in Advancement initiatives Communication & Constituent Engagement Assist with communications and presentations for donors, alumni, churches, faculty, staff, students, and community partners Serve as a primary point of contact for inquiries directed to the Advancement Office Welcome visitors and provide exceptional customer service to internal and external constituents Coordinate communications and information sharing across Advancement teams and university leadership Respond to inquiries with professionalism, diplomacy, and sound judgment Events & Advancement Operations Coordinate planning and logistics for donor events, stewardship activities, campaign initiatives, and Advancement-sponsored gatherings Support university events involving the Vice President and Advancement leadership Partner with Facilities, Campus Dining, University Events, and other departments to support event execution Coordinate office hospitality, supplies, and event-related resources Support donor relations activities in collaboration with Development, Alumni, and Engagement teams Student Employee & Office Coordination Hire, train, schedule, and supervise student employees supporting Advancement office operations Coordinate administrative workflows and shared office processes Assist with onboarding and administrative support activities for Advancement staff Help maintain a welcoming, organized, and professional office environment Office & Financial Administration Oversee daily administrative operations for the Office of University Advancement Manage office supplies, equipment, vendor relationships, and facility-related needs Support budget tracking, expense reconciliation, purchasing card administration, and payment processing through Workday Coordinate personnel action documents, leave reporting, onboarding activities, and administrative workflows Assist with confidential projects and executive-level operational priorities What You Bring Bachelor’s degree or equivalent combination of education and experience Three years of upper-level administrative, executive support, office management, or related experience (five or more years preferred) Exceptional organizational skills and attention to detail Strong written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills Ability to manage multiple priorities and deadlines in a fast-paced environment Demonstrated ability to exercise exceptional discretion when handling confidential donor, personnel, financial, and governance-related information Professional judgment, diplomacy, and discretion when working with senior leaders and external constituents Strong customer service and relationship-building skills Ability to work independently while contributing to a collaborative team environment Proficiency with Microsoft Office Suite, including Outlook, Word, Excel, Teams, and PowerPoint Ability to work occasional evenings and flexible hours during special events and peak Advancement activities Mission Alignment: A personal commitment to Christian faith; enthusiasm for supporting SPU's mission; alignment with the University's Statement of Faith; and willingness to uphold the lifestyle standards of our Christian community Preferred Qualifications Five or more years of executive administrative support experience Experience in higher education, nonprofit organizations, fundraising, donor relations, or advancement operations Experience supporting senior executives, trustees, committees, or donor engagement activities Experience with Workday, CRM systems, event coordination, or advancement-related software platforms Experience coordinating budgets, purchasing, and administrative operations   Compensation & Position Details Salary:  Starting at $69,000 annually, commensurate with experience Status:  Full-time, exempt Department:  University Advancement Reports To:  Vice President for University Advancement Location:  Seattle Pacific University, Seattle, WA (on-campus position) Benefits:  SPU pays employee premiums for medical, dental, and vision insurance; provides HSA/HRA contributions; contributes 7% to a retirement account with immediate vesting (as eligible); and offers a tuition discount program for employees and qualified dependents. Additional Information: How to Apply Please complete the online application through Workday and be prepared to submit the following materials: Resume Cover Letter We look forward to hearing how your unique talents and giftings make you a great fit for this role and how you can contribute to the mission of SPU!"
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Adjunct-Faculty-Pool---Nutrition_JR100350": {
      "title": "Adjunct Faculty Pool - Nutrition",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-13\nPart time\n\nJob Description: Who We Are Seattle Pacific University is a Christian university fully committed to engaging the culture and changing the world by graduating people of competence and character, becoming people of wisdom, and modeling grace-filled community. What we need: The Nutrition Program, part of the College of Applied and Natural Sciences at Seattle Pacific University, seeks adjunct instructors to provide support teaching courses to programs in Excercise Science, Biology, Psychology, Nursing, and pre-professional nutrition training. We seek candidates to teach a variety of nutrition courses. Requirements: PhD or Master's degree in nutrition or relevant field Registered Dietitian preferred, but not required Personal commitment to Christian faith, enthusiasm about supporting  SPU's mission , alignment with the  University's Statement of Faith , and willingness to accept the lifestyle standards of the community. Compensation: $900 - $1,200 per credit, DOE Please direct inquiries to: Catalina Vlad-Ortiz Assistant Professor of Nutrition (206) 281-2986 vladc@spu.edu   Additional Information: The pool of applications at this site will be accessed periodically as specific teaching needs arise. Please complete the online application in Workday. Be prepared to upload a cover letter and CV/resume."
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Main-Campus/Maintenance-Technician-II_JR100335": {
      "title": "Maintenance Technician II",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-06-23\nFull time\n\nJob Description: Who We Are Seattle Pacific University is a Christian university fully committed to engaging the culture and changing the world by graduating people of competence and character, becoming people of wisdom, and modeling grace-filled community. Facility & Project Management supports the university's mission by maintaining a safe, functional, and welcoming campus environment for students, faculty, staff, and guests. Our team plays an essential role in supporting daily campus operations, residential life, academic programs, and major university events through skilled maintenance, responsive service, and a commitment to excellence.   The Facilities Maintenance Technician position supports Seattle Pacific University’s mission by contributing to a Christ‑centered learning and working community committed to academic excellence, service, and engagement with the world. In this role, the employee brings their skills, gifts, and professional expertise to work that matters—serving students, colleagues, and the broader University community in ways that reflect SPU’s values and purpose. To that end, all University employees are required to be aligned with and supportive of SPU's Statement of Faith and its derivative policies and lifestyle expectations.   What We Need Seattle Pacific University is seeking a Maintenance Technician II (two positions available) to support the day-to-day operation of campus facilities through preventive maintenance, repairs, event support, and responsive customer service.This position works across academic, residential, and administrative buildings, helping maintain safe and reliable campus operations. The Maintenance Technician II collaborates closely with other Facilities staff to complete maintenance projects, respond to service requests, support campus events, and assist with emergency operations when needed.The ideal candidate is dependable, safety-conscious, customer-focused, and enjoys hands-on work in a team-oriented environment.This is an in-person position located on SPU's Seattle campus . What You'll Do General Maintenance & Campus Support Perform preventive maintenance and repairs in academic, residential, and administrative buildings Respond to work orders and service requests in a timely and customer-focused manner Troubleshoot maintenance issues and assist in maintaining safe and functional facilities Support seasonal campus activities and special projects as neededCollaborate with Facilities team members to support efficient building operations Equipment, Safety & Teamwork Safely operate and maintain tools, equipment, and materials used in facilities maintenance Inspect work areas and ensure compliance with safety standards and applicable codes Participate in cross-training opportunities to support multiple maintenance disciplines, including carpentry, electrical systems, doors, hardware, and general repairs Contribute to a positive, respectful, and collaborative team environment Event Support Assist with campus event setup and support activities Transport furniture, equipment, and event materials to designated locationsSupport setup and tear-down activities for major university events, including orientation, commencement, and other campus programs Coordinate with campus partners to ensure successful event execution On-Call & Essential Operations Participate in a rotating on-call schedule (approximately seven weeks per year) to respond to urgent maintenance issues outside normal business hours Assist with emergency maintenance needs and campus operations during inclement weather or emergency closures Support a variety of maintenance activities during on-call assignments with guidance from supervisors and team members What You Bring High school diploma or GEDMinimum of three years of experience in building maintenance, construction, facilities operations, or a related field Ability to troubleshoot maintenance issues and follow written and verbal instructions Demonstrated reliability, good judgment, and commitment to workplace safetyAbility to work independently and collaboratively as part of a team Strong customer service and communication skills Valid driver's license with a good driving recordAbility to operate a university service vehicle Ability to perform physical work, including lifting, carrying, climbing ladders, working at heights, and working in indoor and outdoor environments in varying weather conditions Ability to work in confined spaces and access building systems as needed Mission Alignment: A personal commitment to Christian faith; enthusiasm for supporting SPU's mission; alignment with the University's Statement of Faith; and willingness to uphold the lifestyle standards of our Christian community Preferred Qualifications Education or training in facilities maintenance, construction, building systems, or a related trade Experience reading and interpreting blueprintsKnowledge of building, plumbing, electrical, and fire codes Experience working in a college, university, healthcare, hospitality, or other institutional environment Washington State Driver's License Ability to obtain and maintain a 16-hour Asbestos Worker Certification (training provided as needed) Ability to obtain and maintain an 8-hour Lead Renovator Certification (training provided as needed) Compensation & Position Details Salary Range: $29.32-$33.15 per hour, depending on experience Status: Full-time, two positions available Department: Facility Services Location: Seattle Pacific University, Seattle, WA (on-campus position) Benefits: SPU pays employee premiums for medical, dental, and vision insurance; provides HSA/HRA contributions; contributes 7% to a retirement account with immediate vesting (as eligible); and offers a tuition discount program for employees and qualified dependents. All Facilities employees are designated as essential personnel and may be required to work during university closures related to inclement weather or emergency situations. Additional Information: How to Apply Please complete the online application through Workday and be prepared to submit the following materials: Resume Cover Letter We look forward to hearing how your unique talents and giftings make you a great fit for this role and how you can contribute to the mission of SPU!"
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Main-Campus/Senior-Advancement-Officer_JR100265": {
      "title": "Senior Advancement Officer",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-05-12\nFull time\n\nJob Description: Who We Are Seattle Pacific University is a Christian university fully committed to engaging the culture and changing the world by graduating people of competence and character, becoming people of wisdom, and modeling grace-filled community. The Office of University Advancement partners with alumni, parents, and friends to support the mission and priorities of SPU through meaningful philanthropic engagement. Our work is rooted in building lasting relationships, connecting donor passions with institutional needs, and advancing the long-term sustainability and impact of the university. What We Need Seattle Pacific University is seeking a Senior Advancement Officer to cultivate and manage relationships with current and prospective major and planned gift donors. This is a position that plays a key role in securing philanthropic support aligned with SPU’s strategic priorities. Reporting to the Vice President for University Advancement, this role manages a portfolio of donors and prospects capable of making annual gifts of $5,000 or more, multi-year commitments, and planned gifts. Success in this role is measured through portfolio engagement, moves management progress, and achievement of annual fundraising goals. The ideal candidate is relational, strategic, and self-directed, with a demonstrated ability to build trust and inspire generosity. What You’ll Do Major Gift Portfolio Management Manage a portfolio of approximately 80–100 donors and prospects Cultivate, solicit, and steward donors capable of annual gifts of $5,000+, multi-year commitments of $25,000+, and planned gifts of $30,000+ Develop and execute individualized moves management plans for each constituent Conduct regular donor engagement through email, phone, virtual meetings, and in-person visits Donor Strategy & Relationship Building Build strong, trust-based relationships by understanding donor interests and aligning them with university priorities Develop compelling donor proposals that balance institutional needs with donor passions Track and manage all donor activity, communication, and proposals in Raiser’s Edge NXT Partner with Prospect Development to identify and qualify new donor prospects Collaboration & Institutional Engagement Work closely with University Advancement colleagues to achieve shared fundraising goals Partner with university leaders, deans, faculty, and staff to understand and articulate funding priorities Collaborate with Donor Relations to develop and communicate giving opportunities Participate in team meetings, strategy sessions, and Advancement events Donor Engagement & External Relations Attend campus events and activities to deepen understanding of SPU programs and student impact Represent SPU in external settings, building relationships within broader communities Participate in professional development opportunities related to fundraising and advancement Administrative & Planning Responsibilities Manage travel planning, expense reporting, and donor correspondence Maintain accurate and timely documentation of all donor interactions and strategies Monitor progress toward annual goals and adjust strategies as needed What You Bring Bachelor’s degree 2–3 years of professional experience in major gift fundraising, including demonstrated success in donor cultivation and solicitation Ability to manage a portfolio and execute moves management strategies independently Strong interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to engage a wide range of constituents Experience writing compelling donor proposals and impact reports Strong organizational skills and attention to detail This is an on-campus position, but the Senior Advancement Officer will have a willingness and ability to travel within the United States as needed The Senior Advancement Officer resides in the State of WA Mission Alignment: A personal commitment to Christian faith; enthusiasm for supporting SPU’s mission ; alignment with the University’s Statement of Faith ; and willingness to uphold the lifestyle standards of our Christian community Preferred Qualifications 3+ years of relevant fundraising experience, particularly in managing donor portfolios and securing major gifts Demonstrated success securing and managing gifts of $50,000 or more Experience using donor management systems such as Raiser’s Edge or Salesforce Compensation & Position Details Salary: Starting at $106,000 annually, commensurate with experience Status: Full-time, exempt Location: Main Campus in Seattle, WA, with required travel Benefits: SPU pays employee premiums for medical, dental, and vision insurance; provides HSA/HRA contributions; contributes 7% to a retirement account with immediate vesting (as eligible); and offers a tuition discount program for employees and qualified dependents. Additional Information: Please complete the online application through Workday and be prepared to upload the following materials: Resume Cover Letter We look forward to hearing how your unique talents and giftings make you a great fit for this role and how you can contribute to the mission of SPU!"
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Main-Campus/Art---Music-Program-Facilitator_JR100385": {
      "title": "Art & Music Program Facilitator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-15\nFull time\n\nJob Description: Who We Are Seattle Pacific University is a Christian university fully committed to engaging the culture and changing the world by graduating people of competence and character, becoming people of wisdom, and modeling grace-filled community. SPU’s Art and Music programs provide students with opportunities to develop their creative gifts through academic study, artistic practice, performance, and engagement with the broader community. What We Need Seattle Pacific University is seeking an organized, adaptable, and service-oriented Art & Music Program Facilitator to coordinate the day-to-day operations of our Art and Music programs. This position serves as an important point of connection for students, faculty, adjunct faculty, program chairs, and campus partners. The Program Facilitator coordinates academic and departmental processes, facilities and equipment, events, student employees, and administrative activities across both programs. The ideal candidate enjoys working with people, creating order from many moving pieces, and helping academic programs run smoothly behind the scenes. Success in this role requires excellent organization and follow-through, sound judgment, strong communication skills, and the ability to balance competing priorities throughout the academic year. The Art & Music Program Facilitator supports Seattle Pacific   University’s mission by contributing to a Christ‑centered learning and working community committed to academic excellence, service, and engagement with the world. In this role, the employee brings their skills, gifts, and professional expertise to work that matters—serving students, colleagues, and the broader University community in ways that reflect SPU’s values and purpose. To that end, all University employees are required to be aligned with and supportive of   SPU's Statement of Faith and its derivative policies and lifestyle expectations. What You’ll Do Program & Academic Support · Provide day-to-day operational and administrative support for the Art and Music programs, serving as a resource for students, faculty, adjunct faculty, staff, and visitors · Support course scheduling, teaching assignments, registration and enrollment processes, catalog updates, syllabi collection, and classroom supply needs · Assist with Art and Music scholarship processes and monitor scholarship requirements · Process purchasing, invoices, reimbursements, honoraria, travel, and other departmental transactions; maintain departmental records and online resources Facilities, Equipment & Events · Coordinate classrooms, studios, rehearsal and performance spaces, building access, facilities requests, and safety-related needs · Manage program technology, instruments, studio and audiovisual equipment, including inventory, checkout, rentals, repairs, and records · Coordinate logistics and communications for concerts, recitals, exhibitions, and other program events · Support alumni engagement, community outreach, and program-related activities Student Employee Supervision · Hire, train, schedule, supervise, and evaluate student employees · Coordinate work assignments and approve student employee time and payroll submissions Coordination & Communication · Build effective working relationships with students, faculty, administrators, campus departments, and community partners · Coordinate information and requests across both programs and help identify and resolve operational needs · Maintain SharePoint, Canvas, and other departmental systems and resources What You Bring · Bachelor’s degree or an equivalent combination of education and relevant professional experience · Experience providing program, academic, event, office, or operational support · Strong organizational skills and the ability to manage multiple priorities and deadlines with attention to detail · Excellent interpersonal, written, and verbal communication skills · Demonstrated initiative, sound judgment, and ability to solve problems and follow through · Ability to work both independently and collaboratively with students, faculty, staff, and community partners · Proficiency with Microsoft Office and comfort learning administrative and academic technology systems · Ability to supervise and provide direction to student employees · Mission Alignment: A personal commitment to Christian faith; enthusiasm for supporting SPU’s mission ; alignment with the University’s Statement of Faith ; and willingness to uphold the lifestyle standards of our Christian community. Preferred Qualifications · Experience in higher education, an academic department, or an arts-related organization · Experience supporting Art, Music, performing arts, exhibitions, or other creative programs · Experience coordinating events, facilities, specialized equipment, purchasing, or inventory · Experience supervising student employees or other part-time staff · Familiarity with academic processes and systems such as Canvas, SharePoint, or Workday Compensation & Position Details Hourly Rate: $24.36 per hour Status: Full-time, non-exempt Schedule: 40 hours per week during the 10-month appointment Appointment: September through June; this position is off appointment during July and August Location: Seattle Pacific University, Seattle, WA Because this position supports concerts, recitals, exhibitions, and other program activities, occasional evening or weekend work may be required. Benefits: SPU pays employee premiums for medical, dental, and vision insurance; provides HSA/HRA contributions; contributes 7% to a retirement account with immediate vesting (as eligible); and offers a tuition discount program for employees and qualified dependents. Additional Information: How to Apply Please complete the online application through Workday and be prepared to submit: · Resume · Cover Letter We look forward to hearing how your unique talents and giftings make you a great fit for this role and how you can contribute to the mission of SPU!"
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Main-Campus/Senior-Controller-and-Director-of-Finance_JR100391": {
      "title": "Senior Controller and Director of Finance",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-22\nFull time\n\nJob Description: Who We Are Founded in 1891, Seattle Pacific University has a long and distinguished history in Christian higher education. Located just minutes from downtown Seattle, SPU seeks to be a premier Christian university fully committed to engaging the culture and changing the world by graduating people of competence and character, becoming people of wisdom, and modeling grace-filled community. What We Need Seattle Pacific University is seeking an experienced, mission-driven financial leader to serve as its Senior Controller and Director of Finance . Reporting to the Vice President for Business and Finance/CFO, this on-campus position provides strategic and operational leadership for the University’s Finance, Accounting, Treasury, Budget, and Business Development functions. As a key member of the University’s leadership team, the Senior Controller and Director of Finance partners closely with the President, Cabinet, Board of Trustees, and campus leaders to strengthen SPU’s long-term financial health and institutional sustainability. This leader will oversee financial planning and analysis, accounting and reporting, treasury operations, audit and compliance, business operations, and enterprise financial systems while helping the University make thoughtful, data-informed decisions about its resources and priorities. The successful candidate will bring strong financial and technical expertise, strategic perspective, sound judgment, and a collaborative leadership style. This is an opportunity for an accomplished financial professional to have broad institutional impact while developing people, modernizing systems and processes, and advancing the mission of Christian higher education. The Senior Controller and Director of Finance supports Seattle Pacific  University’s mission by contributing to a Christ‑centered learning and working community committed to academic excellence, service, and engagement with the world. In this role, the employee brings their skills, gifts, and professional expertise to work that matters—serving students, colleagues, and the broader University community in ways that reflect SPU’s values and purpose. To that end, all University employees are required to be aligned with and supportive of  SPU's Statement of Faith and its derivative policies and lifestyle expectations.   What You’ll Do Financial Strategy & Stewardship Lead University-wide financial planning and analysis, including multi-year budgeting, forecasting, financial modeling, management reporting, and variance analysis Advise senior leadership and the Board of Trustees on financial performance, emerging risks, capital planning, resource allocation, and strategic investment opportunities Develop financial models and scenario analyses that support institutional planning and long-term sustainability Lead financial stewardship and cost-management initiatives while strengthening budget accountability and transparency across the University Accounting, Treasury & Compliance Oversee accounting and financial operations, including general ledger, accounts payable and receivable, grants accounting, asset management, treasury, liquidity, cash forecasting, debt service, and internal controls Ensure accurate and timely financial reporting in accordance with GAAP, higher education requirements, and applicable federal and state regulations Lead the annual external audit and oversee tax, bond, NCAA, and other regulatory and compliance reporting Support banking and treasury functions for Seattle Pacific University and the Seattle Pacific Foundation Systems & Operational Leadership Serve as executive sponsor and functional owner for Workday Financials, leading governance, optimization, reporting, and future enhancements Develop and strengthen FP&A processes and use technology, automation, and analytics to improve financial decision-making Lead continuous improvement efforts that simplify workflows, strengthen controls, and improve service to campus partners Provide strategic financial and operational oversight for University auxiliary operations, including Conference Services, the SPU Store, Mailing Services, and Vending Services Evaluate existing and new revenue opportunities that support institutional resilience and sustainability Team & University Leadership Lead, mentor, and develop professionals across Finance, Accounting, and Business Development Build a collaborative, service-oriented culture grounded in stewardship, accountability, and operational excellence Partner across academic and administrative areas to strengthen financial understanding and responsible budget ownership Recruit, develop, and retain talented finance and operations professionals What You Bring Bachelor’s degree in Accounting, Finance, Business Administration, or a related field 7–10 years of progressively responsible experience in financial management, accounting, or related areas, including significant leadership responsibility Demonstrated expertise in financial planning, budgeting, forecasting, financial reporting, and analysis Strong knowledge of GAAP, audit standards, internal controls, and applicable regulatory and compliance requirements Proven success leading and developing teams and working collaboratively across a complex organization Experience with modern ERP and financial management systems Exceptional analytical, communication, presentation, and problem-solving skills Ability to translate complex financial information into clear, actionable information for senior leaders and other stakeholders Demonstrated integrity, sound judgment, and commitment to responsible financial stewardship Mission Alignment: A personal commitment to Christian faith; enthusiasm for supporting SPU’s mission; alignment with the University’s Statement of Faith; and willingness to uphold the lifestyle standards of our Christian community. Preferred Qualification s Master’s degree in Business Administration, Finance, Accounting, Finance, Business Administration or a related field CPA, CMA, or other relevant professional certification Financial leadership experience in higher education, a nonprofit organization, or another complex, mission-driven environment Experience with Workday Financials Experience with fund accounting and/or oversight of auxiliary or revenue-generating operations Demonstrated success leading organizational change, financial systems implementations, or significant process-improvement initiatives Compensation & Position Details Status: Full-time, exempt, on-campus position Salary: The anticipated hiring range for this position is $145,000 to $168,000 annually , commensurate with qualifications, skills, and experience. Department: Business & Finance Reports To: Vice President for Business and Finance/CFO Location: Seattle Pacific University, Seattle, WA (on-campus position) This position may require occasional evening and weekend work in support of Board meetings, fiscal year-end activities, and other significant University initiatives. Benefits: SPU pays employee premiums for medical, dental, and vision insurance; provides HSA/HRA contributions; contributes 7% to a retirement account with immediate vesting (as eligible); and offers a tuition discount program for employees and qualified dependents. Additional Information: How to Apply Please complete the online application through Workday and be prepared to submit: · Resume · Cover Letter We look forward to hearing how your leadership, financial expertise, and unique talents and giftings can contribute to the mission and long-term sustainability of Seattle Pacific University."
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Main-Campus/Instructor-or-Assistant-Professor-of-Biology_JR100386": {
      "title": "Instructor or Assistant Professor of Biology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-29\nFull time\n\nJob Description: About Seattle Pacific University Founded in 1891, Seattle Pacific University has a long and distinguished history in Christian higher education. Its comprehensive academic programs serve more than 2,000 undergraduate and graduate students. Located just minutes from downtown Seattle, SPU seeks to be a premier Christian university committed to engaging the culture and changing the world by graduating people of competence and character, becoming people of wisdom, and modeling grace-filled community. Seattle Pacific University seeks faculty who are committed to excellent teaching, meaningful scholarship, and the integration of Christian faith with their academic discipline. We value teachers who mentor students thoughtfully, engage them across varied learning contexts, and help them connect their studies to the wider world.   About Biology The Biology program is part of Seattle Pacific University’s College of Applied and Natural Sciences . Our faculty are committed to excellent undergraduate teaching, meaningful faculty-student engagement, and providing students with opportunities to connect classroom learning with scientific inquiry and hands-on experience. In addition to courses on our Seattle campus, SPU provides distinctive opportunities for field-based teaching and research at the University’s Blakely Island Field Station in Washington’s San Juan Islands. We seek a colleague who will contribute broadly to our Biology curriculum and help students develop the knowledge, skills, and scientific curiosity necessary for their academic, professional, and vocational pursuits.   What We Need Seattle Pacific University invites applications for a regular, full-time, nine-month, non-tenure track faculty position in Biology, beginning September 1, 2027. (Possibly moving to tenure track in the future.) The position may be filled at the rank of Assistant Professor or Instructor; candidates whose highest degree is a master’s may be considered at the rank of Instructor. We seek a broadly trained biologist who can contribute to multiple areas of our undergraduate curriculum and add depth to the program both on our Seattle campus and at the Blakely Island Field Station. We are particularly interested in candidates with expertise in marine biology or biological oceanography. Additional experience in microbiology or molecular biology would strengthen a candidate’s ability to contribute across the curriculum.   What You'll Do Teach upper-division Biology courses within your areas of expertise. Contribute to introductory Biology courses serving both majors and non-majors. Teach and engage students in field-based learning opportunities, including opportunities at the Blakely Island Field Station, as appropriate. Mentor and advise undergraduate students in their academic, vocational, and professional development. Integrate research and scientific inquiry into undergraduate teaching, as appropriate. Provide opportunities for and/or supervise undergraduate student research. Participate in service to the University. What You Bring Graduate degree in Biology or a closely related field. Evidence of, or demonstrated potential for, excellence in undergraduate teaching and mentoring. Ability to teach broadly within an undergraduate Biology curriculum, including introductory courses for majors and non-majors as well as upper-division courses within the candidate’s area of expertise. Ability to support and engage students from a wide range of backgrounds, experiences, and levels of academic preparation. Faith and Learning Integration: Demonstrated ability or commitment to thoughtfully integrate Christian faith with the study and teaching of the biological sciences, helping students consider connections among scientific inquiry, Christian faith, and their broader vocational formation. Faith and Mission Alignment : A personal commitment to Christian faith; enthusiasm for supporting SPU’s mission ; alignment with the University’s Statement of Faith ; and willingness to uphold the lifestyle standards of our Christian community. Preferred : PhD in Biology or a closely related field completed by September 1, 2027. Candidates who are ABD may be considered at the Assistant Professor rank; candidates with a master’s degree may be considered at the Instructor rank. Expertise in marine biology and/or biological oceanography. Additional expertise in microbiology and/or molecular biology. Experience incorporating research or authentic scientific inquiry into undergraduate courses. Experience supervising or mentoring undergraduate research. Interest in field-based teaching and learning, including opportunities at SPU’s Blakely Island Field Station. Compensation and Benefits Assistant Professor: $71,000–$78,500, commensurate with relevant post-secondary teaching experience. Instructor: $62,500–$67,000, commensurate with relevant experience. Faculty salaries at Seattle Pacific University are based on nationally-normed, regionally-adjusted, and discipline-specific data from the College and University Professional Association for Human Resources (CUPA-HR). Specific salary offers will be determined by level of education and relevant experience. All open faculty positions subject to final funding approvals. SPU offers a comprehensive benefits package designed to support employees and their families. Benefits currently include healthcare coverage, retirement benefits, health savings account contributions for eligible employees, tuition benefits for employees and qualified dependents, and other programs that support employee well-being and professional success. Benefit plans and eligibility are subject to change and will be provided at the time of hire.   Application Review: Review of applications will begin November 15, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Applicants from all backgrounds are encouraged to apply. Because our students bring a wide range of backgrounds, experiences, and levels of preparation to the classroom, we value faculty who are committed to supporting and engaging all learners and helping each student flourish. Inquiries may be directed to: Timothy A. Nelson, PhD Dean, College of Applied and Natural Sciences tnelson@spu.edu Additional Information: Application Instructions To apply, submit the online application in Workday. Be prepared to upload a cover letter and CV/Resume . Completion of the application will also include submission of: Personal response to the University's faith statement One page document regarding your teaching philosophy References will be obtained upon request We look forward to learning how your teaching, scientific expertise, and commitment to mentoring students will contribute to the Biology program and enrich the academic community at Seattle Pacific Universit y."
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Main-Campus/Vice-President-for-Business-and-Finance_JR100409": {
      "title": "Vice President for Business and Finance",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-21\nFull time\n\nJob Description: Seattle Pacific University is conducting a national search for its next Vice President for Business & Finance (CFO). The University has engaged FaithSearch Partners to assist with the search. To learn more about the opportunity, please visit FaithSearch Partners here .*  * Do not use the Apply link on the SPU Careers site. To apply you must go through the FaithSearch Partners link above .   Additional Information: For confidential inquiries, nominations, and requests for the full position specifications, please reach out directly to Kimberley Wiedefeld at wiedefeld@ faithsearch partners.com , or, Dr. Andrew Westmoreland at awestmoreland@ faithsearch partners.com ."
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Main-Campus/Facilities-Support-Services-Coordinator_JR100408": {
      "title": "Facilities Support Services Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nFull time\n\nJob Description: Who We Are   Founded in 1891, Seattle Pacific University has a long and distinguished history in Christian higher education. Located just minutes from downtown Seattle, SPU seeks to be a premier Christian university fully committed to engaging the culture and changing the world by graduating people of competence and character, becoming people of wisdom, and modeling grace-filled community.  What We Need Seattle Pacific University’s Facilities department is seeking an organized, service-oriented Facilities Support Services Coordinator to support the day-to-day delivery of Facilities services across campus. This on-campus position coordinates the department’s central stores and inventory functions, including the procurement, receipt, storage, distribution, and tracking of maintenance and custodial supplies. The Coordinator also provides service desk support, assists with maintenance service requests, communicates with campus customers and Facilities staff, and supervises part-time student employees. Through these responsibilities, the position helps keep Facilities resources organized and contributes to timely, responsive service across campus. This role may be a good fit for someone who enjoys a combination of organization, customer service, hands-on operational work, and problem-solving and who takes satisfaction in helping others get the materials and support they need. The Facilities Support Services Coordinator supports Seattle Pacific University's mission by contributing to a Christ-centered learning and working community committed to academic excellence, service, and engagement with the world. In this role, the employee brings their skills, gifts, and professional expertise to work that matters—serving students, colleagues, and the broader University community in ways that reflect SPU's values and purpose. To that end, all University employees are required to be aligned with and supportive of SPU's Statement of Faith and its derivative policies and lifestyle expectations.  What You’ll Do Coordinate central stores and inventory activities. Receive, organize, store, track, and distribute maintenance and custodial supplies, monitor stock levels, maintain accurate records, and help plan for replenishment. Support purchasing and materials coordination. Assist with purchasing, vendor communication, procurement activities, and materials needed to support Facilities operations. Help coordinate Facilities service requests. Work with the Maintenance Manager, Custodial Manager, and other Facilities staff to support work-order intake, dispatching, customer communication, and service tracking using the department’s computerized maintenance management system (CMMS). Provide responsive customer service. Provide service desk coverage, respond to customer questions and service requests, provide status updates, and help resolve routine issues. Supervise student employees. Train and supervise three to five part-time student employees supporting warehouse and service desk functions. Support effective Facilities operations. Work with Facilities staff to evaluate products, materials, and vendor options that meet departmental needs and service objectives. Assist with campus operational needs. As an essential Facilities employee, report for work as required during inclement weather, emergencies, or other situations affecting University operations. What You Bring We’re looking for someone who is dependable, organized, comfortable working with both people and systems, and able to balance several different responsibilities in a service-focused environment. A successful candidate will bring: A high school diploma or GED, or an equivalent combination of education and relevant experience. At least two years of experience in purchasing, warehouse operations, stores/materials operations, or comparable education or experience. Demonstrated commitment to providing high-quality customer service. Sound judgment and the ability to identify issues, ask for assistance when appropriate, and communicate effectively with others. Strong organization, time management, and attention to detail. Strong communication and analytical skills. Experience using Microsoft Office applications, including Teams, Excel, Word, and Outlook. Comfort using computerized systems to maintain records, track inventory, or coordinate work. Experience with a computerized maintenance management system (CMMS), such as TMA, SchoolDude, or Maximo, is helpful. A valid Washington State driver’s license and driving record that meets University requirements. Ability to operate University vehicles ranging from small pickup trucks to larger box trucks. Forklift certification or willingness to complete forklift training. A personal commitment to the Christian faith and affirmation of SPU's Statement of Faith ; enthusiasm for supporting the University's Christian mission ; and willingness to uphold the policies and lifestyle expectations of SPU's Christian community.  Preferred Qualifications The following experience would be helpful, but isn't required: An associate or bachelor’s degree in business or a related field. Familiarity with building materials, maintenance operations, or building system repairs. Experience with billing, invoicing, or records management. Work Environment This is an in-person position located on Seattle Pacific's main campus. This position combines office and service desk responsibilities with warehouse and facilities-related work. Most work takes place indoors, with some responsibilities requiring work outdoors. The position involves moving throughout Facilities work areas and campus spaces, handling supplies and equipment, and occasionally working in varying temperatures and weather conditions. The position requires the ability to lift up to 50 pounds, climb stairs and ladders while carrying supplies or equipment, drive University service vehicles and forklifts (training provided), and work safely around warehouse and facilities equipment. Facilities employees are considered essential personnel and may be required to report to work during inclement weather or other emergencies affecting University operations. Compensation and Benefits Anticipated Hiring Range: $24.00-26.50 per hour, commensurate to experience Status: Full-time, non- exempt  Department: Facility & Project Management  Location: Seattle Pacific University, Seattle, WA  Benefits: SPU offers a comprehensive benefits package designed to support employees and their families. Benefits currently include healthcare coverage, retirement benefits, health savings account contributions for eligible employees, tuition benefits for employees and qualified dependents, and other programs that support employee well-being and professional success. Benefit plans and eligibility are subject to change and will be provided at the time of hire.  Additional Information: How to Apply   Please complete the online application through Workday and be prepared to submit:  Resume  Cover Letter  We look forward to hearing how your unique talents and expereince can contribute to the mission of SPU!"
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Main-Campus/Custodial-Manager_JR100407": {
      "title": "Custodial Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nFull time\n\nJob Description: Who We Are   Founded in 1891, Seattle Pacific University has a long and distinguished history in Christian higher education. Located just minutes from downtown Seattle, SPU seeks to be a premier Christian university fully committed to engaging the culture and changing the world by graduating people of competence and character, becoming people of wisdom, and modeling grace-filled community.  What We Need SPU Facilities is seeking a collaborative, service-oriented Custodial Manager to lead the University’s custodial program and help maintain clean, welcoming, and well-supported campus spaces. This position provides day-to-day leadership for four staff custodians and up to 25 student, seasonal, and temporary employees. The Custodial Manager also oversees contracted custodial services and works closely with the Facilities service desk, maintenance staff, campus partners, and service providers to coordinate work, respond to changing needs, and ensure consistent service quality. This is an excellent opportunity for someone who enjoys developing employees, improving service delivery, solving operational problems, and building positive working relationships across a diverse campus community. The Custodial Manager supports Seattle Pacific University's mission by contributing to a Christ-centered learning and working community committed to academic excellence, service, and engagement with the world. In this role, the employee brings their skills, gifts, and professional expertise to work that matters—serving students, colleagues, and the broader University community in ways that reflect SPU's values and purpose. To that end, all University employees are required to be aligned with and supportive of SPU's Statement of Faith and its derivative policies and lifestyle expectations.  What You’ll Do Lead and develop a diverse custodial team. Hire, train, supervise, and evaluate staff custodians and student, seasonal, and temporary employees. Establish clear expectations, provide coaching, and foster a respectful and supportive workplace. Oversee contracted custodial services. Coordinate quality-control inspections, maintain regular communication with service providers, and address service concerns in a timely and constructive manner. Coordinate campus service requests. Partner with service desk staff and the Maintenance Manager to review, prioritize, and sequence custodial, maintenance, and event-support requests. Build strong campus relationships. Communicate with faculty, staff, students, and other campus partners about service needs, facility operations, schedules, and potential impacts. Monitor quality and improve operations. Use the University’s computerized maintenance management system to coordinate work, follow service requests, and support consistent performance. Manage supplies and equipment. Review and approve custodial and event-support supply and equipment requests while promoting safe, effective, efficient, and environmentally responsible practices. Respond when campus needs change. Provide occasional evening or weekend support. Support the University’s mission. Contribute to a service-focused department committed to operational excellence, environmental stewardship, diversity, reconciliation, and belonging. What You Bring We welcome candidates who have developed their leadership skills through different combinations of education and experience. A competitive candidate will bring: A bachelor’s degree or an equivalent combination of education and relevant experience. One of the following: At least three years of experience in custodial or maintenance management, including client-facing work and oversight of contracted services; or At least one year of supervisory experience plus at least three years of experience in custodial or maintenance operations. Experience interviewing, hiring, training, and supporting employees. Strong customer-service and relationship-building skills. Clear and professional verbal and written communication. The ability to organize competing priorities, respond to deadlines, and make sound decisions in a fast-moving service environment. The ability to address concerns and resolve conflict respectfully and constructively. Working proficiency with Microsoft Excel, Word, Outlook, and Teams. Familiarity with custodial tools, equipment, chemicals, and operating practices. A valid Washington State driver’s license and a driving record that meets University requirements. The ability to drive University service vehicles, including small pickup trucks and box trucks with lift gates. The ability to occasionally adjust working hours to address evening, weekend, emergency, or campus program needs. A personal commitment to the Christian faith and affirmation of SPU's Statement of Faith ; enthusiasm for supporting the University's Christian mission ; and willingness to uphold the policies and lifestyle expectations of SPU's Christian community.  Preferred Qualifications The following experience would be helpful but is not required: Five years of management experience in custodial operations, maintenance, facilities, hospitality, or another service-oriented field. Experience communicating or working with employees who have limited English proficiency. Forklift certification or the ability to obtain it. Ability to safely wear a tight-fitting respirator when required. Work Environment and Schedule This is an in-person position located on Seattle Pacific's main campus, working Monday-Friday first shift business hours. This position combines office-based coordination with regular movement throughout campus facilities. The work includes walking, standing, climbing stairs, inspecting custodial work, and occasional use or handling of custodial equipment and cleaning products. Occasional evening, weekend, and flexible scheduling is required to support campus programs and operational needs. Facilities employees are designated as essential staff and may be required to work when the University closes because of inclement weather or an emergency. Compensation and Benefits Anticipated hiring range: $29.20 - $29.75 per hour, compensation commensurate with experience.  Status: Full-time, non- exempt  Department: Facility & Project Management  Location: Seattle Pacific University, Seattle, WA  Benefits: SPU offers a comprehensive benefits package designed to support employees and their families. Benefits currently include healthcare coverage, retirement benefits, health savings account contributions for eligible employees, tuition benefits for employees and qualified dependents, and other programs that support employee well-being and professional success. Benefit plans and eligibility are subject to change and will be provided at the time of hire.  Additional Information: How to Apply   Please complete the online application through Workday and be prepared to submit:  Resume  Cover Letter  We look forward to hearing how your unique talents and expereince can contribute to the mission of SPU!"
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Main-Campus/Clinical--Counseling---Applied-Psychology_JR100406": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Clinical Psychology",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-16\nFull time\n\nJob Description: About Seattle Pacific University Founded in 1891 and located just minutes from downtown Seattle, SPU is a Christian university committed to building an outstanding faculty whose diverse experiences, perspectives, disciplines, and backgrounds enrich our educational mission . Students at Seattle Pacific University experience world-class education in a world-class city. We welcome students from around the globe to grow together and serve in a community of thoughtful scholarship and outward-focused, living Christian faith.   Christian Mission & Statement of Faith As a Christian university affiliated with the Free Methodist Church-USA, SPU welcomes faculty from a wide range of theologically orthodox Christian traditions who affirm the University's Statement of Faith and are willing to abide by our faith-based conduct expectations. Here is what defines us.   Faculty are expected to contribute to the University's Christian educational mission through their teaching, scholarship, mentoring, and service. We seek educators who are enthusiastic about integrating faith, learning, and vocation and who value the opportunity to serve students within a distinctly Christian academic community.   About the Department of Clinical Psychology In the College of Applied and Natural Sciences (CANS), the Clinical Psychology PhD program at Seattle Pacific University prepares health service psychologists through a balanced scientist-practitioner model and a commitment to evidence-based practice. Through clinical training, research, and service, we prepare students to promote healing, well-being, and reconciliation in the communities they serve. We seek faculty who are committed to effective teaching, meaningful scholarship, strong professional ethics, and student mentorship within an APA-accredited doctoral program. Learn more about the Clinical Psychology program through the Admissions, Outcomes, and Other Data tab on the SPU website.   What We Need The Department of Clinical Psychology at Seattle Pacific University invites applications for one to two non-tenure track faculty positions at the rank of assistant professor in the APA-accredited Clinical Psychology Ph.D. program. These are full-time positions starting September 1, 2027, with a possibility of moving to tenure-track in the future. Exceptional candidates with outstanding qualifications and experience may be considered for an appointment at the rank of Associate Professor.   What You'll Do We seek an individual to teach doctoral coursework, supervise doctoral student research, and provide clinical consultation and supervision in our APA-accredited Clinical Psychology Ph.D. program. While we welcome applications from any area of specialization and research expertise in health service psychology, we have immediate needs for individuals who can teach classes in assessment (e.g., cognitive, personality) and foundational clinical skills. Responsibilities: Major responsibilities include teaching master's and doctoral courses in the clinical psychology Ph.D. curriculum, establishing and maintaining an active program of research, mentoring and supervising students in research and clinical activities, providing statistical mentoring for student dissertations, seeking intramural and/or extramural research funding, and taking on administrative assignments and service within the department, CANS, and the university. What You Bring Earned doctoral degree in Clinical Psychology from an APA-accredited program. Completion of an APA-accredited pre-doctoral internship. Demonstrated ability to teach, mentor, and work effectively with students and colleagues from a wide range of backgrounds, experiences, and perspectives. Personal commitment to Christian faith, enthusiasm about supporting SPU's mission , alignment with the University's Statement of Faith , and willingness to accept the lifestyle standards of the Christian community. Preferred qualifications: Postdoctoral training or other advanced preparation in research and/or a clinical specialty is preferred. A record of scholarly publication and evidence of, or potential for, externally funded research also preferred. Experience and interest in teaching graduate level statistics is preferred. Psychology license in the State of Washington or eligibility for licensure. Compensation and Benefits Anticipated hiring range: Starts at $84,000 up to $92,600 for highly experienced candidates hired at an advanced faculty rank. Faculty salaries at Seattle Pacific University are based on nationally-normed, regionally-adjusted, and discipline-specific data from the College and University Professional Association for Human Resources (CUPA-HR). Specific salary offers will be determined by level of education and years of relevant experience. All open faculty positions subject to final funding approvals. SPU offers a comprehensive benefits package designed to support employees and their families. Benefits currently include healthcare coverage, retirement benefits, health savings account contributions for eligible employees, tuition benefits for employees and qualified dependents, and other programs that support employee well-being and professional success. Benefit plans and eligibility are subject to change and will be provided at the time of hire.     Faculty Community Statement Seattle Pacific University welcomes applications from qualified candidates of all backgrounds and experiences. Because our students bring a wide range of backgrounds, experiences, perspectives, and levels of preparation to the classroom, we value faculty who are committed to supporting student success, engaging all learners, and contributing to an academic community characterized by respect, intellectual curiosity, and Christian faith.   Inquiries should be addressed to: Jenny Lee Vaydich, PhD Associate Professor & Chair, Research Psychology (MS) and Clinical Psychology (PhD) School of Psychology, Family, & Community vaydichj@spu.edu   Application Instructions Review of applications will begin December 1, 2026, and continue until the position is filled. Only applications received through the online portal will be considered. The application package requires: Fully completing the SPU faculty application questionnaire. The application questionnaire includes: A faith statement of approximately one page A teaching philosophy statement of approximately one page Other items: Resume/CV Cover Letter A narrative of research trajectory Additional supporting materials may be submitted when available, such as a representative syllabus, teaching evaluations, or recent publications The above documents are uploaded in the same place as the resume, at the beginning of the application process. You may return/back page to the upload space as long as the application has not been submitted. References upon request Additional Information: We look forward to hearing about how you will bring your unique talents and giftings to serve our amazing students, and the ways you look forward to contributing to the mission of SPU!"
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Main-Campus/Event-Specialist---Undergraduate-Admissions--Temporary--Part-Time-_JR100402": {
      "title": "Event Specialist - Undergraduate Admissions (Temporary, Part-Time)",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nPart time\n\nJob Description: Who We Are Founded in 1891, Seattle Pacific University has a long and distinguished history in Christian higher education. Located just minutes from downtown Seattle, SPU seeks to be a premier Christian university fully committed to engaging the culture and changing the world by graduating people of competence and character, becoming people of wisdom, and modeling grace-filled community. What We Need Seattle Pacific University is seeking an organized, resourceful, and detail-oriented Event Specialist to support major events hosted by Undergraduate Admissions, including the Counselor Fly-In, Distinguished Scholars Award Competition, and Admitted Student Preview. This position has a unique appointment period: Approximately 5 hours per week beginning in November through the end of January. Approximately 20 hours per week beginning in February of 2027 and continuing through April 2027. This temporary, part-time position will play a key role in planning and executing a special conference event, coordinating logistics, vendors, campus partners, and event participants to ensure a welcoming and well-organized experience. The ideal candidate enjoys bringing events to life, thrives on managing details and deadlines, and is comfortable working with a variety of stakeholders. This is an excellent opportunity for an experienced event professional seeking a flexible, project-based assignment in a collaborative university environment. What You'll Do Event Planning & Coordination Coordinate planning and day-of logistics for the Counselor Fly-In and support other major Undergraduate Admissions events as needed. Develop event schedules, run sheets, timelines, and logistical plans, including speakers, panels, catering, facilities, lodging, and transportation. Coordinate event registration, participant communications, and other arrangements to ensure a positive attendee experience. Anticipate and troubleshoot logistical challenges, making adjustments to keep events running smoothly. Vendor & Campus Collaboration Communicate with vendors, negotiate event services and contracts within established University guidelines, and coordinate delivery of services. Collaborate with Undergraduate Admissions, Conference Services, Sodexo, Facilities, the Office of the President, and other campus partners. Coordinate communications and assignments for event participants, staff, and volunteers. Support on-site event setup, execution, and follow-up activities. What You Bring Bachelor's degree or an equivalent combination of education and relevant professional experience. One to two years of experience coordinating events, conferences, or similar programs. Strong organizational and project-management skills, with the ability to manage multiple details, deadlines, and priorities. Excellent written and verbal communication skills and the ability to build productive relationships with vendors, colleagues, and event participants. Demonstrated ability to work independently, anticipate needs, solve problems, and adapt to changing circumstances. Proficiency with Microsoft Office applications, including Outlook, and familiarity with CRM or event-management systems. Ability to work on-site during events, including extended periods of standing and walking, and coordinating staff and volunteers, with or without reasonable accommodation. Mission Alignment: A personal commitment to Christian faith; enthusiasm for supporting SPU's mission; alignment with the University's Statement of Faith; and willingness to uphold the lifestyle standards of our Christian community. Preferred Qualifications Experience planning and coordinating large conferences, corporate events, or multi-day programs. Experience coordinating events in higher education or a similar organizational setting. Experience working with vendors, negotiating services, and coordinating event budgets and logistics. Familiarity with event registration, scheduling, or customer relationship management systems. Compensation & Position Details Hourly Rate: $30.00 per hour Status: Temporary, part-time, non-exempt Department: Undergraduate Admissions Location: Seattle Pacific University, Seattle, WA (on-campus position) Anticipated Appointment: November 2026 through end of April 2027 Work Schedule: Approximately 5 hours per week beginning in November through the end of January. Approximately 20 hours per week beginning in February of 2027 and continuing through April 2027. The schedule will vary based on event planning needs and may include occasional evening or weekend hours during major events.  Additional Information: How to Apply Please complete the online application through Workday and be prepared to submit: Resume Cover Letter We look forward to hearing how your event-planning experience, organizational talents, and unique giftings can contribute to memorable and successful Undergraduate Admissions events at SPU!"
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Main-Campus/IT-HelpDesk-Manager_JR100404": {
      "title": "IT HelpDesk Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\nFull time\n\nJob Description: Who We Are Founded in 1891, Seattle Pacific University has a long and distinguished history in Christian higher education. Located just minutes from downtown Seattle, SPU seeks to be a premier Christian university fully committed to engaging the culture and changing the world by graduating people of competence and character, becoming people of wisdom, and modeling grace-filled community. SPU's Information Technology team supports this mission by delivering secure, reliable, and forward-looking technology that enables students, faculty, and staff to do their best work. Our IT team values service, collaboration, accountability, and continuous improvement, with a focus on delivering practical solutions that make a meaningful difference across the University. What We Need Seattle Pacific University is seeking a technically skilled, service-oriented Help Desk Manager to lead the daily operations of our IT Support Desk and deliver high-quality technology support to students, faculty, and staff. This on-campus position oversees Help Desk services, supervises and mentors a team of more than 10 student employees, and provides hands-on technical expertise for complex support issues. Working closely with the Director of Technology Support Services, the Help Desk Manager helps shape service strategies, improve processes, and ensure that campus technology support is responsive, effective, and aligned with the University's needs. The ideal candidate combines strong technical troubleshooting abilities with a passion for developing people, improving systems, and delivering exceptional customer service. They are proactive, organized, and comfortable balancing day-to-day operational demands with opportunities for innovation and continuous improvement. The on-site Help Desk Manager supports Seattle Pacific University's mission by contributing to a Christ-centered learning and working community committed to academic excellence, service, and engagement with the world. In this role, the employee brings their skills, gifts, and professional expertise to work that matters—serving students, colleagues, and the broader University community in ways that reflect SPU's values and purpose. To that end, all University employees are required to be aligned with and supportive of SPU's Statement of Faith and its derivative policies and lifestyle expectations. What You'll Do Help Desk Operations & Technical Support Lead daily IT Support Desk operations, ensuring timely, professional, and effective support for students, faculty, and staff. Monitor service requests, ticket queues, escalations, staffing coverage, and service performance to maintain consistent service quality. Provide hands-on technical support for complex issues involving computers, software, classroom technology, audiovisual equipment, and other campus systems. Identify opportunities to improve Help Desk processes, support tools, workflows, and the overall customer experience. Team Leadership & Development Supervise, train, schedule, and mentor a team of 10+ student employees. Establish clear expectations, provide performance feedback, and foster a positive, accountable, and service-focused team environment. Develop and maintain training materials, onboarding resources, and knowledge base documentation for student employees and end users. Technology Services & Collaboration Partner with the Director of Technology Support Services and colleagues to improve Help Desk services and support evolving campus technology needs. Maintain accurate, accessible technical documentation and promote consistent troubleshooting practices. Ensure Help Desk operations follow University IT policies, security standards, and data protection requirements. Collaborate with campus departments to resolve service challenges, provide technology guidance, and support effective use of University systems. What You Bring At least three years of experience in IT support, technical customer service, or a related technology support environment. Demonstrated experience supervising, training, or mentoring employees, preferably in a technical support or customer service setting. Strong technical troubleshooting skills across personal computing hardware, software, and common workplace technology. Experience managing service requests, troubleshooting workflows, and customer support processes. Excellent interpersonal and communication skills, with the ability to explain technical concepts clearly to users with varying levels of technical knowledge. Strong organizational, problem-solving, and decision-making skills, with the ability to prioritize competing needs and resolve issues effectively. Demonstrated initiative, accountability, and ability to work collaboratively across technical and nontechnical teams. Commitment to excellent customer service, continuous improvement, and responsible technology stewardship. Mission Alignment: A personal commitment to the Christian faith and affirmation of SPU's Statement of Faith; enthusiasm for supporting the University's Christian mission; and willingness to uphold the policies and lifestyle expectations of SPU's Christian community. Preferred Qualification s Bachelor's degree in Information Technology, Computer Science, Information Systems, or a related field, or an equivalent combination of education and professional experience. Experience managing or coordinating an IT Help Desk or service desk operation. Familiarity with ITIL principles, service management frameworks, or related certifications. Experience supporting classroom technology, audiovisual systems, or technology in an academic environment. Experience working in higher education or another complex, service-oriented organization. Experience developing technical documentation, training programs, and knowledge management resources. Compensation & Position Details Anticipated Hiring Pay Rate: $36.00 - $44.00 per hour, commensurate with experience. Status: Full-time, non-exempt Department: Information Technology Reports To: Director of Technology Support Services Location: Seattle Pacific University, Seattle, WA (on-campus position) Benefits: Seattle Pacific University offers a comprehensive benefits package designed to support employees and their families. Benefits for eligible employees include medical, dental, and vision insurance; University contributions to a health savings or health reimbursement account; employer-paid life and long-term disability insurance; retirement benefits, including a University contribution; tuition discounts for employees and eligible family members; and paid time off, including vacation, sick leave, and University holidays. Additional benefits include an Employee Assistance Program and wellness resources. Benefits and eligibility are subject to applicable University policies and plan provisions. Additional Information: How to Apply Please complete the online application through Workday and be prepared to submit: · Resume · Cover Letter We look forward to hearing how your unique talents and giftings make you a great fit for this role and how you can contribute to the mission of SPU!"
    },
    "https://spu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/SPU/job/Main-Campus/Senior-Director-for--University-Communications_JR100401": {
      "title": "Senior Director for  University Communications",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-14\nFull time\n\nJob Description: Who We Are   Founded in 1891, Seattle Pacific University has a long and distinguished history in Christian higher education. Located just minutes from downtown Seattle, SPU seeks to be a premier Christian university fully committed to engaging the culture and changing the world by graduating people of competence and character, becoming people of wisdom, and modeling grace-filled community.  University Communications plays a vital role in telling SPU's story, strengthening its institutional identity, and connecting prospective students, families, alumni, donors, and the broader community with the University's distinctive mission and educational experience.  What We Need   Seattle Pacific University is seeking an accomplished, strategic, and creative Senior Director of University Communications to lead institutional marketing and communications efforts that strengthen the SPU brand, support enrollment and advancement priorities, and elevate the University's visibility and reputation.  Leading a talented eight-member University Communications team, the Senior Director oversees a broad portfolio that includes institutional branding, digital marketing, website strategy, social media, media relations, publications, design, photography, videography, and executive communications. This position serves as a trusted communications partner to University leadership, translating institutional priorities into compelling messages and coordinated strategies that engage key audiences.  The ideal candidate is both a visionary leader and a skilled communications practitioner—an exceptional writer and storyteller who brings strategic insight, creativity, sound judgment, and a collaborative approach to their work. They thrive in a dynamic environment, build strong relationships across departments, and are committed to delivering measurable results.  The Senior Director of University Communications supports Seattle Pacific University's mission by contributing to a Christ-centered learning and working community committed to academic excellence, service, and engagement with the world. In this role, the employee brings their skills, gifts, and professional expertise to work that matters—serving students, colleagues, and the broader University community in ways that reflect SPU's values and purpose. To that end, all University employees are required to be aligned with and supportive of SPU's Statement of Faith and its derivative policies and lifestyle expectations.  What You'll Do   Strategic Communications & Brand Leadership   Lead University-wide communications and marketing strategies that advance institutional priorities, strengthen SPU's brand, and engage internal and external audiences.  Serve as a strategic communications advisor to University leadership, including the President's Cabinet and Office of the President, on executive messaging, media relations, and crisis communications.  Guide institutional storytelling, messaging, editorial standards, and content development across digital, print, and multimedia platforms.  Write, edit, and develop compelling communications that reflect SPU's distinctive mission, values, and educational experience.  Digital Marketing & Enrollment Communications   Direct the University's website and digital communications strategy, ensuring an engaging user experience, consistent messaging, and effective integration with digital advertising.  Oversee social media, photography, videography, and digital content strategies that strengthen audience engagement and institutional visibility.  Partner closely with Undergraduate and Graduate Admissions to develop integrated marketing and communications strategies that support enrollment goals.  Use analytics, audience insights, and performance measures to evaluate effectiveness and continuously improve marketing outcomes.  Team Leadership & University Partnerships   Lead, mentor, and develop an eight-member communications team responsible for marketing, web, design, multimedia, writing, publications, and media relations.  Collaborate with University Advancement to develop alumni and parent communications, fundraising appeals, case statements, and other materials that inspire engagement and philanthropic support.  Build productive relationships with faculty, staff, and campus leaders to ensure coordinated communications and excellent service to University partners.  Operations & Project Management   Oversee communications projects, production schedules, and workflows to ensure quality, timeliness, and alignment with institutional priorities.  Manage the departmental budget, including forecasting, expense oversight, and responsible allocation of resources.  Direct relationships with external vendors, agencies, and partners supporting the University's marketing, advertising, and communications initiatives.      What You Bring   Bachelor's degree in communications, marketing, public relations, business, or a related field.  At least 10 years of progressively responsible experience in communications, marketing, public relations, or a related field, including at least three years of supervisory experience.  Demonstrated success developing and executing strategic communications and marketing initiatives, preferably in higher education or a similarly complex organization.  Exceptional writing, editing, storytelling, and verbal communication skills, including proficiency with AP style.  Strong leadership skills with demonstrated success building, mentoring, and managing collaborative, high-performing teams.  Experience overseeing digital marketing, website content, social media, and integrated communications campaigns.  Ability to translate institutional priorities and audience insights into compelling messaging, actionable strategies, and measurable outcomes.  Strong project management, organizational, and budget management skills, with the ability to balance multiple priorities and deadlines.  Familiarity with content management systems, CRM platforms, digital analytics tools, and emerging AI applications for marketing and communications.  Excellent interpersonal skills, sound judgment, and the ability to build trusted relationships with senior leaders and stakeholders across an organization.  Mission Alignment: A personal commitment to the Christian faith and affirmation of SPU's Statement of Faith ; enthusiasm for supporting the University's Christian mission ; and willingness to uphold the policies and lifestyle expectations of SPU's Christian community.  Preferred Qualifications   Master's degree in communications, marketing, business, or a related field.  Experience in Christian higher education or another faith-based organization, particularly one integrating Christian faith and learning.  Degree from a college or university that integrates Christian faith and learning such as a Council for Christian Colleges & Universities member institution.  Experience supporting enrollment marketing, advancement communications, executive communications, or institutional crisis communications.  Advanced experience in one or more specialized areas, including digital media, web strategy, photography, videography, or social media marketing.  Experience with Sitecore, WordPress, Jira, Slate CRM, Workday, or AI-powered communications tools.  Compensation & Position Details   Salary: Anticipated hiring range $117,000 - $119,000, compensation commensurate with experience.  Status: Full-time, exempt  Department: University Communications  Location: Seattle Pacific University, Seattle, WA  Benefits: SPU offers a comprehensive benefits package designed to support employees and their families. Benefits currently include healthcare coverage, retirement benefits, health savings account contributions for eligible employees, tuition benefits for employees and qualified dependents, and other programs that support employee well-being and professional success. Benefit plans and eligibility are subject to change and will be provided at the time of hire.  Additional Information: How to Apply   Please complete the online application through Workday and be prepared to submit:  Resume  Cover Letter  We look forward to hearing how your leadership, creativity, communications expertise, and commitment to SPU's Christian mission can help tell the University's story and strengthen its impact!"
    }
  },
  "rows": {
